==Latin==
{{wikipedia|lang=la}}

===Etymology===
The word ''haruspex'' is composed of {{term||haru-|intestines|lang=la}} and {{term||-spex|lang=la}}, which is derived from {{m|la|speciō||to observe, watch}}.<ref>{{R:Nocentini|hw=aruspice}}</ref> Compare Faliscan {{m|xfa|harispex}}.

According to Nocentini<ref>{{R:Nocentini|hw=aruspice}}</ref> the first part stems from {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} {{m|ine-pro|*ǵʰer-||intestine}}, whence also Latin {{term|hariolus|lang=la}}, {{term|hernia||hernia|lang=la}}, and it is cognate to Ancient Greek {{term|χορδή|lang=grc}}, Proto-Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*garnō||intestines}} (whence German {{term|Garn|lang=de}}) and to Lithuanian {{m|lt|žarnà||intestine}}.

The second part of ''haru-spex'' can also be found in the Latin word {{term|auspex|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-pronunc|haruspex}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|haruspex||haruspicis|m|third}}

# [[diviner]] who reads from the intestines of sacrificial animals; one who practices [[haruspicy]].

